#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355104 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a28c4320-e2dd-5195-81ac-b910c2328691" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Repeated Integral Defined Over a Type I Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4bddc1a-885f-5d34-853a-77b9098528c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355106} {:answer/id #uuid "d1afaa30-d5c2-5c40-8f89-49a349cb720f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355107} {:answer/id #uuid "03900c69-17e5-58c1-8061-c49c807171c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355108} {:answer/id #uuid "1f865459-eacc-5938-a32e-6434f61920b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355109} {:answer/id #uuid "ff895d4c-11c5-5c72-a4c6-67d415f6fb03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03900c69-17e5-58c1-8061-c49c807171c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355108} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee0745fc-045c-583f-9d68-cf03880cc771" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "67ca505d-008f-5389-9f32-c45ef0e44690" :question/math-academy-id "q-132276" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dy\\,dx=$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type I region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x[\\,y\\,]_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,dx & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-2) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355111 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a28c4320-e2dd-5195-81ac-b910c2328691" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Repeated Integral Defined Over a Type I Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5543ef31-dcfe-55a5-b8a8-7f23167b449f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355113} {:answer/id #uuid "0bc036ed-9155-546c-94b7-f82b9bc76f3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355114} {:answer/id #uuid "9e227874-43d6-577f-a15e-ffd10970d5c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355115} {:answer/id #uuid "f5da3ce4-be8d-5bc0-9194-6ec9b2ac4f15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355116} {:answer/id #uuid "4cdf106d-f431-53b9-a023-ce338c1c7135" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355117}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5543ef31-dcfe-55a5-b8a8-7f23167b449f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355113} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6f5c200-2c7a-555e-ace9-8f508595cf05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355112}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3630c4f5-1f80-56c5-89c5-e4637aed4fd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-37683" :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1/x}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx.$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type I region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1/x}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}[{\\int }_{x}^{1/x}y\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{x}^{1/x}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-{x}^{2})dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)\\,dx & =\\frac{1}{2}{[-\\frac{1}{3{x}^{3}}-x]}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{1}{3}+1-(\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{1}{2})] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{11}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355118 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "69f6b2a7-7a3a-5c2c-90db-5aa321784048" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Double Integral Defined Over a Type I Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97187c1b-4fdb-52bb-ae26-c9ba925d0b7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355120} {:answer/id #uuid "291b2acb-cfc4-59da-a935-030002db3eab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355121} {:answer/id #uuid "8a1d76eb-0e11-5b85-aa7c-f80308bc113b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355122} {:answer/id #uuid "6515893c-d11a-5f45-9a7d-5e2ff1d7a773" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355123} {:answer/id #uuid "b706b36c-5a51-5e2a-ac59-a839878bb4ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355124}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6515893c-d11a-5f45-9a7d-5e2ff1d7a773" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355123} :answer-field/id #uuid "30936431-098b-5922-9211-7dfc9d49666f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355119}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4bff3979-2b39-5332-9bb6-24c5c18decd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-37865" :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral\n${∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,dA,$\nwhere $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the line\n$y=1-x$ and the coordinate axes." :question/worked-solution "Let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067291/assets/95ebd0a4eb874972b459e53eecf1d57650f63284698fcad967c09620ac01cd99.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I region.\n\n- The line $y=1-x$ intercepts the $x$-axis at $x=1.$\nSo, the $x$-limits are \n$0\\le x\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067291/assets/d048f20f2299722cee598cc1005237627ecdd7643e996b269f26b5a096f01f2f.png)\n\nAs a result, the integral is defined over the following type I region: \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1-x}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,dA={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{-x+1}\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,dy]\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-\\frac{1}{1+x+y}]}_{0}^{-x+1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{1}{1+x+(1-x)}-(-\\frac{1}{1+x+0}))\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{1+x})dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{1+x})dx & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-(0+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355125 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "853b2770-5057-50aa-9c15-7426cf4cc6a4" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Not Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "658c3a22-9fde-53bb-876c-4f996b3c123c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{4}^{2x}{y}^{2}\\sqrt{x}\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355127} {:answer/id #uuid "4e2413d2-9ed5-5ebc-9751-3b7e7a13602c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{x}^{4}{y}^{2}\\sqrt{x}\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355128} {:answer/id #uuid "6c5ebaa1-79cb-58bf-a53b-1e3d6362a18d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{2x}^{4}{y}^{2}\\sqrt{x}\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355129} {:answer/id #uuid "eaf1b0ac-c3c9-5844-b262-6b5ba8f3d5a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-2x}^{4}{y}^{2}\\sqrt{x}\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355130} {:answer/id #uuid "72de0563-0ef0-5c1b-8353-f350474c7871" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{x/2}^{4}{y}^{2}\\sqrt{x}\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355131}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c5ebaa1-79cb-58bf-a53b-1e3d6362a18d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{2x}^{4}{y}^{2}\\sqrt{x}\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355129} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3c4a7a2-8c4a-5d47-82d3-57cf2fe097cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355126}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "911b35a5-c875-5dda-a9fb-91433cdde5c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-40747" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067291/assets/ce364dc7c6c4fc5e229fdeb0809b3779bf1052c60d956deb742731f34e54562d.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the lines $y=2x,$ $x=0,$ and $y=4,$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\sqrt{x}\\,dA=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =4 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x$-limits are \n$0\\le x\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067291/assets/54f366679684823341bab818459ce5fc884ebbdd2376e800b781d1b832c4ba64.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,2x\\le y\\le 4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{2x}^{4}{y}^{2}\\sqrt{x}\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355132 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "69f6b2a7-7a3a-5c2c-90db-5aa321784048" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Double Integral Defined Over a Type I Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "047ac638-bb64-5d8d-a039-75ba521457f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355134} {:answer/id #uuid "7beab571-e611-52ae-a353-f67f703458eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355135} {:answer/id #uuid "87cb4dc0-5245-5ff8-b694-a082217b6fd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355136} {:answer/id #uuid "9e740309-62af-54a7-a695-21e843849d5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355137} {:answer/id #uuid "1e214645-5d14-56fa-aced-71c8ce96b13d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355138}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e740309-62af-54a7-a695-21e843849d5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355137} :answer-field/id #uuid "57789863-d478-5b9e-b4b5-7222e4bd270b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "745899d2-5260-5f7a-af3f-e49c255ef5ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-81776" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067291/assets/6accbfcccdf910acca3aab9f1a12d0ab35c4da683fe78d6ca2d7ada88f28767c.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\,dA,$\nwhere $D$ is the finite region above the $x$-axis enclosed by the curve $y=1-{x}^{2}$ and the line $x=0.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $D$ is a type I region.\n\n- We already know the intersection of the curve with the line $y=0.$\nSo, the $x$-limits are \n$0\\le x\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067291/assets/db0dd47348fb2f9a97c9e0514f74adae05830de7e03f1aea2886eaa95701200e.png)\n\nAs a result, the integral is defined over the following type I region: \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1-{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\,dA={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{x}^{2}}{x}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{x}^{2}}{x}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-{x}^{2}}dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}[y]_{0}^{1-{x}^{2}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}(1-{x}^{2}-0)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{x}^{4})\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{x}^{4})\\,dx & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{5}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
